# Click limits (`max_clicks`)

A short link can carry an optional click limit. After the link has been followed `max_clicks`
times, further requests to the short code answer `410 Gone`. Links without a limit keep working
exactly as before. Change reference: @@CHANGE_REFERENCE@@.

## Using it

Create a link with an optional `max_clicks`, an integer from @@MIN_VALUE_COMMAS@@ to
@@MAX_VALUE_COMMAS@@:

```http
POST /api/v1/links
X-API-Key: <your key>

{"url": "https://example.com/campaign", "max_clicks": 3}
```

The response (and the link details, and every item of the list) shows the limit and the
remaining allowance:

```json
{
  "code": "Ab3dE5g",
  "short_url": "https://sho.example.com/Ab3dE5g",
  "original_url": "https://example.com/campaign",
  "created_at": "2026-01-15T12:00:00Z",
  "expires_at": null,
  "status": "active",
  "click_count": 0,
  "max_clicks": 3,
  "remaining_clicks": 3
}
```

| Situation | Result |
|---|---|
| `max_clicks` omitted or `null` | Unlimited link. The JSON has no `max_clicks` / `remaining_clicks` keys at all. |
| Redirect within the limit | `302`; `click_count` and `remaining_clicks` are exact immediately. |
| Redirect after the limit | `410` with error code `link_gone` and the message "The link has reached its click limit."; the link status is `exhausted` and `remaining_clicks` is `0`. |
| Disabled or expired link | `410` as before; a refused request never consumes a click. |
| `max_clicks` of 0, below @@MIN_VALUE_COMMAS@@, above @@MAX_VALUE_COMMAS@@, not an integer, `true` | `422`; nothing is stored. |

## API compatibility

The change is additive (`breaking: false`): `max_clicks` is an optional request field, the two
response fields are optional and omitted for unlimited links, and `exhausted` is a new value of
the response `status`. A request without `max_clicks` has the same idempotency fingerprint as
before, so a retry that crosses the deployment still replays. `docs/api.md` is generated from the
OpenAPI schema (`python -m shortener.apidoc`); regenerate it if it was not part of the change.

## How the limit is enforced

`LinkRepository.consume_click` runs one statement,

```sql
UPDATE links SET click_count = click_count + 1
WHERE code = ? AND disabled_at IS NULL AND (max_clicks IS NULL OR click_count < max_clicks)
```

and `LinkService.resolve` treats `rowcount == 0` as "no click left". SQLite serialises writers, so
of N concurrent requests at most the remaining allowance sees `rowcount == 1`, whatever the number
of processes. The redirect cache keeps only the immutable limit of a link (so unlimited links keep
the cached fast path); the allowance is never read from it, and the entry is dropped when the limit
is reached. The background click writer still stores the analytics rows of limited links but no
longer adds to their `click_count`, because the redirect path already did.

